public @Nullable LocalDate getRegistrationDate() {
```
- `sharesHousehold` (lines 218-222) — nullable, no constraints. Documented as: "When true, permits creating this owner even if another owner has the same last name and address." (Note: no code in this POST path currently reads `sharesHousehold` — no duplicate-household check is enforced server-side despite the description.)

## 4. Custom validator + @InitBinder

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/advice/OwnerFieldsValidationAdvice.java` — a `@ControllerAdvice` implementing `org.springframework.validation.Validator`, registered via `@InitBinder`. It runs alongside the `@Valid` Bean Validation and **mutates the payload** (normalizes address and telephone in place):

```java
@ControllerAdvice
public class OwnerFieldsValidationAdvice implements Validator {

    @InitBinder
    void registerOwnerFieldsValidator(WebDataBinder binder) {
        binder.addValidators(this);
    }

    @Override
    public boolean supports(Class<?> clazz) {
        return OwnerFieldsDto.class.isAssignableFrom(clazz);
    }

    @Override
    public void validate(Object target, Errors errors) {
        OwnerFieldsDto owner = (OwnerFieldsDto) target;
        rejectIfBlank(errors, "firstName", owner.getFirstName());
        rejectIfBlank(errors, "lastName", owner.getLastName());
        owner.setAddress(AddressNormalizer.normalize(owner.getAddress()));
        rejectIfBlank(errors, "address", owner.getAddress());
        rejectIfBlank(errors, "city", owner.getCity());
        rejectIfBlank(errors, "telephone", owner.getTelephone());
        normalizeTelephone(errors, owner);
    }

    private void rejectIfBlank(Errors errors, String field, String value) {
        if (value == null || value.isBlank()) {
            errors.rejectValue(field, "required", "must not be blank");
        }
    }

    private void normalizeTelephone(Errors errors, OwnerFieldsDto owner) {
        if (owner.getTelephone() == null) {
            return;
        }
        String e164 = E164Telephone.toE164(owner.getTelephone());
        if (e164 != null) {
            owner.setTelephone(e164);
        } else {
            errors.rejectValue("telephone", "telephone.invalid", "must be a valid E.164 telephone");
        }
    }
}
```

Two package-private helpers it calls:

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/advice/AddressNormalizer.java` (trims/collapses whitespace, upper-cases, expands ST→STREET, RD→ROAD, AVE→AVENUE; null-safe):
```java
static String normalize(String address) {
    if (address == null) {
        return null;
    }
    return address.strip().replaceAll("\\s+", " ").toUpperCase()
        .replaceAll("\\bST\\b", "STREET")
        .replaceAll("\\bRD\\b", "ROAD")
        .replaceAll("\\bAVE\\b", "AVENUE");
}
```

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/advice/E164Telephone.java` (strips spaces/dashes/brackets, assumes +61 when no country code, drops a leading 0, requires 8-15 digits):
```java
static String toE164(String raw) {
    String cleaned = raw.replaceAll("[ ()\\-]", "");
    String digits = cleaned.startsWith("+") ? cleaned.substring(1)
        : "61" + (cleaned.startsWith("0") ? cleaned.substring(1) : cleaned);
    return digits.matches("\\d{8,15}") ? "+" + digits : null;
}
```

Ordering caveat: the `@Pattern`/`@Size` Jakarta constraints in the DTO run against the ORIGINAL input; the `OwnerFieldsValidationAdvice` then normalizes address (to upper-case, may exceed nothing) and telephone (to `+61...` E.164). The persisted/echoed telephone is the normalized E.164 value, and the DTO `@Pattern` for telephone does not exist (only `@Size`), while the entity enforces E.164 (see below).

There is also an unrelated custom validator pair `PetAgeValidator` / `PetAgeValidation` under `.../rest/validation/`, but those apply to pets, not owners.

## 5. Service layer, repository, entity callbacks/constraints

Service call `clinicService.saveOwner(owner)` — `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/service/ClinicServiceImpl.java` lines 232-237:
```java
@Override
@Transactional
public void saveOwner(Owner owner) throws DataAccessException {
    ownerRepository.save(owner);
}
```
The controller also calls `clinicService.findAllOwners()` (lines 126-129) to feed `CustomerCode`:
```java
@Override  // (readOnly transactional)
public Collection<Owner> findAllOwners() throws DataAccessException {
    return ownerRepository.findAll();
}
```

Repository: active profile is `spring-data-jpa` (`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/application.properties` line 20: `spring.profiles.active=h2,spring-data-jpa`), so the implementation is Spring Data's generated proxy for `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/repository/springdatajpa/SpringDataOwnerRepository.java` (extends `OwnerRepository` + `Repository<Owner,Integer>`; `save` is auto-implemented by Spring Data, not custom-coded). The `@Profile("jpa")` `JpaOwnerRepositoryImpl.save` (persist-vs-merge on null id) is NOT active under the default profile.

Entity lifecycle callbacks: **none** — there are no `@PrePersist` / `@PreUpdate` / `@PostPersist` methods anywhere in the model package.

Entity-level validation + id generation:

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java` (lines 36-59) — constraints and a setter with hidden side effects:
```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "owners")
public class Owner extends Person {
    @Column(name = "address")
    @NotEmpty
    private String address;

    @Column(name = "city")
    @NotEmpty
    private String city;

    @Column(name = "telephone")
    @NotEmpty
    @Pattern(regexp = "^\\+[0-9]{8,15}$", message = "Phone number must be in E.164 format")
    private String telephone;

    @Column(name = "email")
    private String email;

    @Column(name = "registration_date")
    private LocalDate registrationDate;

    @Column(name = "customer_code")
    private String customerCode;
```
Side-effecting setters on the entity (lines 76-88):
```java
public void setRegistrationDate(LocalDate registrationDate) {
    this.registrationDate = BusinessDay.onOrNextBusinessDay(
        registrationDate == null ? LocalDate.now() : registrationDate);
}
...
public void setEmail(String email) {
    this.email = email == null ? null : email.toLowerCase();
}
```
`BusinessDay.onOrNextBusinessDay` (`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/util/BusinessDay.java`) rolls Sat/Sun forward to Monday. So on create, `registrationDate` defaults to today (rolled to next business day) if omitted, and email is lower-cased. These setters run when MapStruct maps the DTO into the entity.

Parent `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Person.java` (lines 31-37) adds `@NotEmpty firstName` / `@NotEmpty lastName`, plus derived getters `getDisplayName()` and `getInitials()` (used only in the response DTO). Base `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/BaseEntity.java` provides `@Id @GeneratedValue(strategy = IDENTITY) Integer id` and `isNew()`.

## 6. Mappers, events, logging, other side effects

Mapper `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/OwnerMapper.java` (MapStruct, `@Mapper(uses = PetMapper.class)`):
